[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
## TL;DR  

| Feature | What you used | What you need for MapLibre | How to switch |
|---------|---------------|----------------------------|---------------|
| **Drawing tools** | `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw` | **Works unchanged** (v1.4.0 +). Only a small “alias” tweak may be required in your bundler. | Keep the same package, just import it after you create a `MapLibreGL.Map`. |
| **Geocoder UI** | `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder` | Use the **MapLibre-specific fork** – `@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder` (or the community `maplibre-gl-geocoder`). | Replace the import, point it at a geocoding endpoint you have access to (Mapbox, Pelias, OpenCage, etc.). |

Below is a step-by-step guide that shows the exact changes you’ll need to make in a typical Webpack/Parcel/Vite project.

---

## 1. Why the change matters  

* **Mapbox GL JS v2** is no longer open-source (it uses a proprietary license).  
* **MapLibre GL JS** is the open-source continuation of the v1 API.  
* Most third-party plugins that only talk to the *core GL API* (e.g. `mapbox-gl-draw`) keep working, but any plugin that imports `mapbox-gl` directly will break unless you tell your bundler to resolve that import to `maplibre-gl`.

---

## 2. Drawing shapes – `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw`

### 2.1 Does it work with MapLibre?  

Yes. `mapbox-gl-draw` only depends on the **Mapbox GL JS public API** (the `Map` object, `addSource`, `addLayer`, etc.). Those APIs are 100 % compatible in MapLibre GL JS v2.x, so the plugin works out-of-the-box **as long as you use a recent version (≥ 1.4.0)**.

### 2.2 What you have to do  

| Step | Action | Code |
|------|--------|------|
| **A. Install the same package** | No change – keep `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw` in `package.json`. | `npm i @mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw` |
| **B. Make sure the draw code runs after the MapLibre map is created** | Same pattern you already use. | ```js<br>import MapLibreGL from 'maplibre-gl';<br>import MapboxDraw from '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw';<br><br>const map = new MapLibreGL.Map({ … });<br>const draw = new MapboxDraw({<br>  displayControlsDefault: false,<br>  controls: { polygon: true, trash: true }<br>});<br>map.addControl(draw);<br>``` |
| **C. (Optional) Alias `mapbox-gl` → `maplibre-gl` for bundlers** | Some bundlers (Webpack, Vite, Rollup) will try to resolve `mapbox-gl` when they see a *type-only* import inside `mapbox-gl-draw`. Adding an alias avoids a “module not found” error. | **Webpack**<br>`resolve: { alias: { 'mapbox-gl': 'maplibre-gl' } }`<br>**Vite**<br>`resolve: { alias: [{ find: 'mapbox-gl', replacement: 'maplibre-gl' }] }` |
| **D. CSS** | The CSS file shipped with the draw plugin is unchanged. | ```html<br><link href="https://unpkg.com/@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw/dist/mapbox-gl-draw.css" rel="stylesheet">``` |

### 2.3 Gotchas  

| Issue | Why it happens | Fix |
|-------|----------------|-----|
| **`mapbox-gl` peer-dependency warning** | `mapbox-gl-draw` lists `mapbox-gl` as a peer. | Ignore the warning, or add the alias above. |
| **Missing `Map` type in TypeScript** | The typings reference `mapbox-gl`. | Install `@types/mapbox-gl` (they work for MapLibre) **or** add a simple `declare module 'mapbox-gl'` that re-exports `maplibre-gl`. |
| **Custom styles that reference Mapbox-specific sprite names** | Draw uses the default `mapbox-gl-draw` sprite sheet. | No change needed; the sprite sheet is bundled with the plugin. |

---

## 3. Search box – Geocoder

### 3.1 The original plugin (`@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder`)  

* It imports `mapbox-gl` directly, so it will throw a “module not found” error when you replace `mapbox-gl` with `maplibre-gl`.  
* It also ships with a **Mapbox-only UI** that expects a Mapbox access token and the Mapbox Geocoding API.

### 3.2 Recommended replacement  

**`@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder`** (officially maintained by the MapLibre community) – a drop-in fork that:

* Resolves `mapbox-gl` to `maplibre-gl` internally.  
* Lets you point the geocoder at **any** compatible geocoding endpoint (Mapbox, Pelias, OpenCage, Maptiler, etc.).  
* Keeps the same UI/UX (the CSS is identical).

> **Alternative** – If you already have a custom geocoder UI, you can keep `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder` and just add the same alias trick (`mapbox-gl → maplibre-gl`). However, you’ll still be tied to the Mapbox Geocoding API unless you override the `geocoder` option.

### 3.3 How to switch  

| Step | Action | Code |
|------|--------|------|
| **A. Install the MapLibre fork** | Replace the Mapbox package. | `npm i @maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder` |
| **B. Import the new module** | Same API surface. | ```js<br>import MapLibreGL from 'maplibre-gl';<br>import MapLibreGeocoder from '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder';<br>``` |
| **C. Choose a geocoding endpoint** | • **Mapbox** – keep using your token.<br>• **Open-source** – e.g., Pelias (`https://api.geocode.earth/v1/search`) or Maptiler (`https://api.maptiler.com/geocoding/{query}.json`). | ```js<br>const geocoder = new MapLibreGeocoder({<br>  accessToken: '<YOUR_MAPBOX_TOKEN>', // optional if you use Mapbox<br>  // OR for a non-Mapbox service:<br>  // url: 'https://api.geocode.earth/v1/search',<br>  // forwardGeocode: async (query) => { … }, // custom fetch wrapper if needed<br>  maplibregl: MapLibreGL,<br>  placeholder: 'Search places…',<br>});<br>map.addControl(geocoder);<br>``` |
| **D. CSS** | Same stylesheet as the Mapbox version. | ```html<br><link href="https://unpkg.com/@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder/dist/maplibre-gl-geocoder.css" rel="stylesheet">``` |
| **E. (Optional) Alias for the old package** | If you still want to keep the old import path for a quick test, add the same alias as for draw. | `resolve: { alias: { '@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder': '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder' } }` |

### 3.4 Using a non-Mapbox geocoder (example with Pelias)

```js
import MapLibreGL from 'maplibre-gl';
import MapLibreGeocoder from '@maplibre/maplibre-gl-geocoder';

const map = new MapLibreGL.Map({ container: 'map', style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json' });

const geocoder = new MapLibreGeocoder({
  // No Mapbox token needed
  forwardGeocode: async (query) => {
    const response = await fetch(`https://api.geocode.earth/v1/search?text=${encodeURIComponent(query)}&api_key=YOUR_PELIAS_KEY`);
    const data = await response.json();

    // Convert Pelias response to the format the UI expects
    return {
      features: data.features.map(f => ({
        type: 'Feature',
        geometry: f.geometry,
        properties: {
          // The UI reads `text` for the label
          text: f.properties.label,
          // Optional: `place_name` is also used by the UI
          place_name: f.properties.label,
        },
        // `center` is required for the UI to zoom to the result
        center: f.geometry.coordinates,
      })),
    };
  },
  maplibregl: MapLibreGL,
  placeholder: 'Search…',
});

map.addControl(geocoder);
```

> **Tip:** If you use a third-party geocoder that already follows the Mapbox Geocoding API spec (Maptiler, OpenCage’s “Mapbox-compatible” endpoint, etc.), you can simply pass `accessToken` **or** `url` and skip the custom `forwardGeocode` wrapper.

