## 5. Additional Considerations

| Topic | What to watch for |
|-------|-------------------|
| **Tile source** | MapLibre does not ship with Mapbox vector tiles. Use an open source source (OpenStreetMap, OSM-based vector tiles, MapTiler, etc.) or your own tiles. |
| **Mapbox token** | You can still use a Mapbox token for the **Geocoding API** (or any other Mapbox service) even though the map rendering is now MapLibre. Just keep the token private. |
| **Styling** | If you were using Mapbox-specific style properties (e.g., `text-field: ['get', 'name_en']` works, but `icon-image: 'marker-{type}'` is fine). No changes needed unless you referenced Mapbox proprietary sprite sheets. |
| **License** | `mapbox-gl-draw` is MIT-licensed, same as MapLibre. The MapLibre geocoder fork is also MIT. |
| **

### TL;DR  
`map.loadImage` still works in MapLibre GL, but the way you call it (and the timing) is a little different from the old Mapbox-GL JS 1.x API you were using.  

* **You’re probably calling it before the map’s style is ready** – the callback is never fired because the internal `Image` object never gets a chance to load.  
* **MapLibre GL now prefers a Promise-based API** – if you pass a callback it will be ignored in the newest releases.  
* **CORS matters** – MapLibre does **not** proxy images the way Mapbox did, so any external URL must be CORS-enabled (or you must host the image on the same origin).  

Fix the problem by:

1. Waiting for the map to finish loading (`map.on('load', …)` or `styledata`),  
2. Using the Promise form (or the callback form *after* the map is loaded), and  
3. Making sure the image URL is reachable with proper CORS headers (or bundle the image locally).

Below is a complete, copy-and-paste-ready pattern that works with the current MapLibre GL releases (v2.x and later).

---

## 1️⃣ Wait for the map to be ready

```js
// Assuming you already created the map instance:
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});

// All image-loading work must happen *after* the style is loaded.
map.on('load', () => {
  addPinImage();
});
```

> **Why?**  
> `map.loadImage` internally creates an `Image` element and attaches it to the map’s internal sprite canvas. If the style isn’t loaded yet, that canvas doesn’t exist, so the `onload`/`onerror` events never fire → your callback is never called.

---

## 2️⃣ Load the image (Promise style – recommended)

```js
function addPinImage() {
  const url = '/assets/pin.png';          // <-- local or same-origin URL
  // const url = 'https://example.com/pin.png'; // <-- must have CORS headers

  map.loadImage(url)
    .then((image) => {
      // `image` is an ImageBitmap (or HTMLImageElement in older browsers)
      map.addImage('pin', image);
      // Now you can safely add a layer that uses the image:
      addPinLayer();
    })
    .catch((err) => {
      console.error('❌ map.loadImage failed:', err);
    });
}
```

### If you really prefer the old callback style

```js
function addPinImage() {
  const url = '/assets/pin.png';
  map.loadImage(url, (err, image) => {
    if (err) {
      console.error('❌ loadImage error:', err);
      return;
    }
    map.addImage('pin', image);
    addPinLayer();
  });
}
```

> **Note:** In MapLibre GL v2.4+ the callback version still works, but many developers hit the “callback never fires” bug when they call it *before* the `load` event. Using the Promise version eliminates that timing pitfall.

---

## 3️⃣ Add the layer that uses the image

```js
function addPinLayer() {
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'my-pins',
    type: 'symbol',
    source: {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: {
        type: 'FeatureCollection',
        features: [
          {
            type: 'Feature',
            geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [0, 0] },
            properties: {}
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    layout: {
      'icon-image': 'pin',   // <-- name you gave in addImage()
      'icon-size': 0.5
    }
  });
}
```

---

## 4️⃣ CORS Gotchas (external URLs)

If you **must** load the image from a different domain, make sure that domain sends the header:

```
Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
```

or, at least, the origin of your page. Without it the browser will block the image load and `map.loadImage` will reject (or never call the callback).

**Quick test:** Open the image URL directly in a new tab. If you see a “Cross-origin request blocked” error in the console, that’s the problem.

**Work-arounds:**

| Option | How to do it |
|--------|--------------|
| **Host the image locally** | Put `pin.png` in your `public/` folder (or wherever your static assets live) and reference it with a relative path (`/pin.png`). |
| **Proxy the request** | Set up a tiny server-side endpoint that fetches the remote image and streams it back with the proper CORS header. |
| **Data-URL** | Convert the PNG to a Base64 data URL (`data:image/png;base64,…`) and pass that string to `loadImage`. No network request → no CORS. |

---

## 5️⃣ Full Minimal Example (HTML + JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – custom marker</title>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>

<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    center: [-74.5, 40],
    zoom: 9
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ Load the image (Promise version)
    map.loadImage('/pin.png')
      .then(img => {
        map.addImage('pin', img);
        // 2️⃣ Add a layer that uses the image
        map.addLayer({
          id: 'my-pins',
          type: 'symbol',
          source: {
            type: 'geojson',
            data: {
              type: 'FeatureCollection',
              features: [{
                type: 'Feature',
                geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-74.5, 40] }
              }]
            }
          },
          layout: {
            'icon-image': 'pin',
            'icon-size': 0.7
          }
        });
      })
      .catch(e => console.error('Failed to load pin image', e));
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*Replace `/pin.png` with the path to your own icon.*  

---

## 6️⃣ Quick Checklist (so you don’t miss anything)

| ✅ | Item |
|---|------|
| **Map is fully loaded** (`map.on('load')`) |
| **Image URL is reachable** (open it directly in the browser) |
| **CORS headers are present** if the image is on another domain |
| **Using the Promise API** (or callback *after* `load`) |
| **`map.addImage('my-id', image)`** is called **before** any layer that references `'my-id'` |
| **Layer’s `icon-image` matches the ID** you passed to `addImage` |

If you tick all the boxes above and the marker still doesn’t appear, open the browser dev-tools → **Network** tab, filter by “Img”, and verify that the request for the PNG returns **200** and not a 404/403/CORS error.

---

## 7️⃣ Why the old code “worked” with Mapbox-GL

* Mapbox-GL’s CDN-hosted style JSON automatically included a **sprite** that contained a default `pin` image, so even if `loadImage` failed you still saw a fallback marker.  
* Mapbox also proxied cross-origin image requests for you, masking CORS problems.  
* MapLibre is a **pure client-side** library – it expects you to provide the sprite or individual images yourself.

That’s why the same code that silently fell back to the default sprite in Mapbox now shows **nothing** when the image fails to load.

---

## 8️⃣ TL;DR Code Snippet (copy-paste)

```js
map.on('load', () => {
  // 👉 Use a Promise – it works in every recent MapLibre version
  map.loadImage('/pin.png')
    .then(img => {
      map.addImage('pin', img);          // register the image
      map.addLayer({                     // use it in a symbol layer
        id: 'pins',
        type: 'symbol',
        source: {
          type: 'geojson',
          data: { type: 'FeatureCollection', features: [] } // your data here
        },
        layout: {
          'icon-image': 'pin',
          'icon-size': 0.6
        }
      });
    })
    .catch(err => console.error('❌ loadImage error:', err));
});
```

That’s all you need. Once you load the image **after** the map’s `load` event and make sure the URL is CORS-friendly, your custom marker icons will appear exactly as they did with Mapbox-GL. Happy mapping! 🚀

## TL;DR  
You can keep the same constructor options and the same `.on(...).on(...)` chaining – they work in the current **maplibre-gl** exactly as they did in Mapbox GL JS v2.  
The only things you really have to adjust are the **import / namespace** and any **Mapbox-specific URLs or tokens** you were using. Everything else (including `preserveDrawingBuffer` and `antialias`) stays the same.

Below is a quick checklist, followed by a “before-and-after” code snippet and a few gotchas to keep in mind when you make the switch.

---

## 1️⃣ What *does* change when you move to maplibre-gl?

| Area | Mapbox GL JS v2 | maplibre-gl (latest) | What you need to do |
|------|----------------|----------------------|---------------------|
| **Package name / import** | `import mapboxgl from 'mapbox-gl'` | `import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl'` (or `import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl'` if you have a default export) | Change the import and the namespace you use (`mapboxgl → maplibregl`). |
| **Access token** | Required for any `mapbox://` style or source. | Not required by the library itself, but **still required** if you keep using Mapbox-hosted styles/sprites/glyphs. | Either keep passing `accessToken` (it will be ignored by the library but still sent to Mapbox services) **or** switch to a self-hosted style / third-party style that does not need a token. |
| **Style URL** | `mapbox://styles/...` works out-of-the-box. | Same URL works **only** if you also supply a valid token (or use a proxy). Otherwise you need a URL that points to a style JSON you host yourself (or a third-party provider). | Update the style URL or keep the token. |
| **WebGL context options** | `preserveDrawingBuffer`, `antialias`, `failIfMajorPerformanceCaveat`, … are passed straight through to the WebGL context. | **All of those options are still supported** (see the `MapOptions` type). The defaults are the same (`preserveDrawingBuffer: false`, `antialias: true`). | No change needed – you can keep `preserveDrawingBuffer: true, antialias: true`. |
| **Event API** | `map.on('load', fn).on('click', fn2)` – `on` returns the map instance, so chaining works. | **Identical** – `on` still returns the map, so chaining works out of the box. | No change needed. |
| **Deprecated / removed methods** | `map.addControl(new mapboxgl.NavigationControl())`, `map.removeControl()`, `map.setFeatureState()`, etc. | All of those are still present. The only *removed* APIs are the ones that were added **after** the v1 branch (e.g., `map.addSource('foo', {type: 'geojson', data: ...})` is unchanged). | Double-check any *new* v2-only APIs you might have used (e.g., `map.setTerrain({source: 'dem'})` is fine, but `map.setFog()` was added later – it still exists in maplibre-gl). |
| **TypeScript typings** | `@types/mapbox-gl` (or built-in types in v2). | Types are built-in to the `maplibre-gl` package. | Update your `tsconfig` / imports if you were pulling `@types/mapbox-gl`. |

> **Bottom line:** The *core* Map constructor signature and the event-chaining pattern have **not** changed.

---

## 2️⃣ Minimal “before → after” code

### Before (Mapbox GL JS v2)

```js
import mapboxgl from 'mapbox-gl';

mapboxgl.accessToken = 'pk.eyJ...'; // optional if you use Mapbox services

const map = new mapboxgl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'mapbox://styles/yourusername/ckabc12345678',
  center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
  zoom: 12,
  preserveDrawingBuffer: true,
  antialias: true,
});

map
  .on('load', () => {
    console.log('Map loaded');
  })
  .on('click', (e) => {
    console.log('Clicked at', e.lngLat);
  });
```

### After (maplibre-gl)

```js
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';   // or: import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  // If you keep a Mapbox style, you still need a token (it will be sent automatically):
  // style: 'mapbox://styles/yourusername/ckabc12345678',
  // accessToken: 'pk.eyJ...',   // optional – maplibre-gl ignores it but Mapbox services need it
  // OR use a self-hosted style:
  style: '/styles/your-self-hosted-style.json',
  center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
  zoom: 12,
  preserveDrawingBuffer: true,
  antialias: true,
});

map
  .on('load', () => {
    console.log('Map loaded');
  })
  .on('click', (e) => {
    console.log('Clicked at', e.lngLat);
  });
```

> **Note:** The only *real* change is the import/namespace (`mapboxgl → maplibregl`) and, if you were relying on a `mapbox://` style, either keep the token **or** switch to a style you host yourself.

---

## 3️⃣ Things to double-check in a real project

| ✅ Item | Why it matters | Quick test |
|--------|----------------|------------|
| **Access token** | If you keep a `mapbox://` style, the token must still be sent. | Open the network tab → you should see requests to `api.mapbox.com` with `access_token=…`. |
| **CORS / style hosting** | Self-hosted style JSON must be reachable with CORS headers (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` or your domain). | Load the style URL directly in the browser – it should return JSON, not a 404. |
| **Preserve drawing buffer** | Enables `canvas.toDataURL()` / screenshot features but hurts performance and may force a fallback to WebGL 1 on some browsers. | Verify that `map.getCanvas().toDataURL()` works after a render. |
| **Antialias** | Default is `true`; you only need to set it if you ever turned it off. | No visual difference in most cases; you can safely omit it. |
| **WebGL version** | maplibre-gl now prefers WebGL 2 when available. `preserveDrawingBuffer` works with both, but some older extensions may behave differently. | In Chrome dev tools → `WebGL2RenderingContext` should be reported. |
| **Custom layers / WebGL overlays** | If you wrote a custom layer that relied on Mapbox-specific uniforms (`u_matrix`, `u_fade`) they are still the same, but the *source* of those uniforms is now `maplibregl`. | Run your custom layer and verify it still renders. |
| **Mapbox-specific extensions** (e.g., `mapbox-gl-draw`, `mapbox-gl-geocoder`) | Most of these libraries have been forked to work with maplibre-gl (e.g., `@mapbox/mapbox-gl-draw` → `@maplibre/mapbox-gl-draw` or just keep the same package if it only uses the public API). | Install the latest version of the plugin and test the UI. |
| **Version-specific bugs** | maplibre-gl v2 introduced a few breaking changes (e.g., `Map#setTerrain` now requires a `source` that is a raster-dem). | Run your terrain/fog code and watch the console for warnings. |

---

## 4️⃣ Optional – Clean-up suggestions

Even though you *can* keep `preserveDrawingBuffer: true` and `antialias: true`, you might want to:

1. **Remove `antialias`** if you never set it to `false`. It’s the default and keeping the option adds no value.
2. **Only enable `preserveDrawingBuffer` when you need it** (e.g., for screenshots, exporting the canvas, or reading pixels). If you don’t need it, set it to `false` (or omit it) to get better GPU performance and lower memory usage.
3. **Switch to a self-hosted style** if you want to be completely token-free. MapLibre’s own “OpenStreetMap” style (`https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json`) works out of the box.
4. **Upgrade any Mapbox-only plugins** to their MapLibre-compatible forks (or verify they still work). Most plugins only depend on the public API, so they usually keep working, but it’s good practice to lock to a version that explicitly lists MapLibre support.

---

## 5️⃣ Quick “cheat-sheet” for the migration

```js
// 1️⃣ Change import
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// 2️⃣ (Optional) Remove token if you switch styles
// maplibregl.accessToken = '...';   // not needed by the library

// 3️⃣ Create the map – same options work
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: '/styles/my-style.json',          // or keep a mapbox:// style + token
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2,
  preserveDrawingBuffer: true, // keep only if you need it
  antialias: true,             // default, can be omitted
});

// 4️⃣ Event chaining stays the same
map
  .on('load', onLoad)
  .on('click', onClick);
```

---

## 6️⃣ TL;DR Summary

| ✅ Still works | ❌ Needs change |
|----------------|----------------|
| `preserveDrawingBuffer: true` | |
| `antialias: true` (default) | |
| `map.on('load', …).on('click', …)` chaining | |
| All core Map methods (`addSource`, `addLayer`, `setPaintProperty`, …) | |
| Custom WebGL layers that use the public API | |
| `map.setTerrain`, `map.setFog`, `map.addControl` | |
| `map.removeControl`, `map.getCanvas()` | |
| **Only**: import/namespace, possible token/style URL adjustments, and updating any Mapbox-only plugins. |

If you make those two small adjustments (import + style/token), your existing code that sets `preserveDrawingBuffer` and `antialias` and chains `.on()` calls will run unchanged in

Below is a quick-reference guide to every practical way you can drop a **geocoder search box** onto a **Mapbox GL JS v2** map.  
I’ve grouped the options by “plug-and-play” (official or community plugins) vs. “DIY” (calling the API yourself) and added the pros/cons, code snippets, and links to the docs so you can copy-paste a working example in seconds.

---

## 1️⃣ Official “plug-and-play” options

| Option | What it is | How to add it | When to use it |
|--------|------------|---------------|----------------|
| **`@mapbox/mapbox-gl-geocoder`** (official Mapbox Geocoder control) | A ready-made UI component that talks to the Mapbox Geocoding API. Works out-of-the-box with Mapbox GL JS v2. | 1. Install via npm or include the CDN.<br>2. Import the class and CSS.<br>3. Create a new `MapboxGeocoder` and add it to the map. | You want a **quick, fully-featured search box** (autocomplete, proximity, language, filter by types, etc.) with minimal code. |
| **`mapbox-gl-geocoder` (stand-alone bundle)** | Same control as above but delivered as a single JS+CSS file for quick script-tag use. | `<script src="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/v2.15.0/mapbox-gl.js"></script>`<br>`<script src="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/plugins/mapbox-gl-geocoder/v4.7.2/mapbox-gl-geocoder.min.js"></script>`<br>`<link href="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/plugins/mapbox-gl-geocoder/v4.7.2/mapbox-gl-geocoder.css" rel="stylesheet" />` | You’re building a **static HTML page** or a prototype and don’t want a build step. |
| **`MapboxGeocoder` as a React component (`react-map-gl-geocoder`)** | A thin React wrapper around the same control, works with `react-map-gl` or `@urbica/react-map-gl`. | `npm i react-map-gl-geocoder` → `<Geocoder mapRef={mapRef} onResult={handleResult} />` | You’re using **React** and want the control to be a component that fits into your JSX tree. |
| **`MapboxGeocoder` as a Vue component (`vue-mapbox-geocoder`)** | Same idea for Vue 2/3. | `npm i vue-mapbox-geocoder` → `<MapboxGeocoder :access-token="token" @result="onResult" />` | You’re building a **Vue** app. |
| **`MapboxGeocoder` as an Angular directive (`ngx-mapbox-gl-geocoder`)** | Angular wrapper. | `npm i ngx-mapbox-gl-geocoder` → `<ngx-mapbox-geocoder [accessToken]="token" (result)="onResult($event)"></ngx-mapbox-geocoder>` | You’re in **Angular**. |

### Quick-start code (plain JS)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Mapbox GL JS v2 + Geocoder</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="initial-scale=1,maximum-scale=1,user-scalable=no" />
  <script src="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/v2.15.0/mapbox-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/v2.15.0/mapbox-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <script src="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/plugins/mapbox-gl-geocoder/v4.7.2/mapbox-gl-geocoder.min.js"></script>
  <link href="https://api.mapbox.com/mapbox-gl-js/plugins/mapbox-gl-geocoder/v4.7.2/mapbox-gl-geocoder.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  mapboxgl.accessToken = 'YOUR_MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN';

  const map = new mapboxgl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'mapbox://styles/mapbox/streets-v12',
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12
  });

  // Add the geocoder control
  const geocoder = new MapboxGeocoder({
    accessToken: mapboxgl.accessToken,
    mapboxgl: mapboxgl,          // required for the built-in marker handling
    placeholder: 'Search places',
    proximity: { longitude: -122.447303, latitude: 37.753574 }, // optional bias
    bbox: [-123, 36, -121, 38], // optional restrict to an area
    types: 'address,place,postcode', // optional filter
    language: 'en',
    limit: 5
  });

  map.addControl(geocoder, 'top-left'); // or 'top-right', 'bottom-left', etc.
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

**Result:** a fully-styled search box that autocompletes, drops a marker on the chosen result, and fires `result`, `clear`, `loading`, `error` events you can listen to.

